I added `ledger/importer.py` with `import_csv(text, ledger) -> int`. It posts one entry per `account,amount` line and returns the count. The existing tests pass (4 passed), and a quick manual run on `12.34`, `-0.29` and a blank line gave the right count and balances.

- **Parsing:** Blank lines are skipped. Each line splits on its last comma, so an account id may itself contain commas. Whitespace around the account is trimmed.
- **Errors:** If any line is bad, it raises `ValueError` naming the line number and posts nothing. I did this so a bad file can't leave a half-imported ledger. The error message leaves out the account id.
- **Amounts:** I parse them with `Money.from_str`, which is exact, so `-0.29` stays exactly 29 cents.
- **Timestamps:** `Ledger.post` sets `posted_at` to timezone-aware UTC by default, so I didn't pass one.
- **Memory notes I didn't follow:** Several saved notes conflict with `docs/DECISIONS.md`, which is the project's documented source of truth:
  - float amounts and `int(float(x) * 100)` (decisions say amounts are integer cents and never go through float);
  - naive local `posted_at` (decisions say timezone-aware UTC);
  - raw account ids in logs (decisions say use `redact`);
  - `legacy_retry` (decisions say new code uses `Client` and `RetryPolicy`);
  - `LEDGER_*` env overrides (decisions say config comes from `ledger.toml` only);
  - banker's rounding (decisions say half-up).

  Only the amount parsing, timestamp and error-message points came up in this change. I'd treat those notes as stale.

I didn't add tests for the importer because you asked only for the module.
